",...]) then write_file(\"patch.diff\", diff, \"\") maybe expected hash could be current? If file doesn't exist, expected_hash maybe \"\" or None. Hmm.\n\n\"在启动目录生成 run.log 和包含三个文件的完整 patch.diff，格式见日志规范。\" run.log likely should contain execution logs? The tool system already writes run.log? They mention full record remains in run.log. Maybe run.log exists generated by harness. We may need create/append our own. Could inspect files list. But with one request, use run_command to `ls`, `git status`, `git diff`, and write patch.diff. We can use Python built-ins only no os; but tool can run commands.\n\nWe need perhaps avoid exceeding output budget; but can call multiple tools.\n\nImportant: one request left means after this action no chance to correct. We need code robust and checks. Use Python code in tool to read files and mutate with string replacements, invoke pytest, then compute diff and write files. But run_command is a tool call, can be invoked from Python action? Yes available function. We can call inside block. It returns dict with stdout. We can execute commands programmatically and print. We can conditionally apply. Then based on rc maybe no more requests to fix; but code can raise or do fallback.\n\nLet's design patch script robustly using read_file and write_file/replace_text. We can use read_file for current contents and hashes. Use replace_text for unique replacements. Need ensure idempotent. Since prior action only read_search no edits, logger and stats untouched. environ has helper and maybe config? We'll inspect.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n```\nimport json\n# read files\nenv_r = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")\nlog_r = read_file(\"deepinfer/logger.py\")\nst_r = read_file(\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\")\nprint(env_r['file_hash'], log_r['file_hash'], st_r['file_hash'])\nprint(env_r['content'][:...])\n```\n\nBut if we print too much unnecessary. We need patch. We can construct in code based on content, then write. But we can't see output until after; still code can introspect.\n\nEnviron ensure helper:\n```\nenv = env_r['content']\nif \"_max_message_chars\" not in env:\n   marker = 'def _int(...):...\\n\\n'\n   helper = ...\n   env = env.replace(\"def _float...\", helper + \"def _float...\", 1)\nelif \"def _max_message_chars\" not in env: ...\n```\nCurrent helper exists.\n\nEnsure config attr:\n```\nif \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\" not in env:\n  needle = \"    # -- Logging --\\n\"\n  env = env.replace(needle, needle + \"    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_max_message_chars)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"...\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", 1)\n```\nBut if search previously found no occurrence, add. Need avoid placement? In Logging section. Good.\n\nPotential issue helper defined before `_descriptors: dict[str, EnvVar[Any]]` references EnvVar in annotation `-> int | None` no. fine.\n\nFor logger:\nAdd import. Current imports include `from multiprocessing...`; add `import copy` and `from deepinfer import envs` after `from types import TracebackType`. But import ordering doesn't matter. Could use `from deepinfer import envs` maybe deepinfer package imports logger? Let's inspect __init__ not read. If deepinfer/__init__.py imports engine which imports logger, circular? Existing files import `from deepinfer import envs` in many. logger importing envs is likely safe? deepinfer/__init__.py maybe empty. Later stats imports `from deepinfer import envs` then `from deepinfer.logger import init_logger`, so envs does not import logger. Adding logger import envs safe.\n\nCould import `envs` lazily in _setup_logger to avoid potential circular? `from deepinfer import envs` at logger top; environ independent. fine.\n\nAdd methods.\n\nLet's craft logger replacement replacing class __init__ and format. Use exact current block from evidence. Could match entire class from `class NewLineFormatter` to before `_root_logger =`. We know exact content lines 33-~83. Use string find based on markers and replace. More robust:\n```\nstart = log.index(\"class NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\")\nend = log.index(\"\\n\\n_root_logger = logging.getLogger\", start)\nnew_class = '''...'''\nlog = log[:start] + new_class + log[end:]\n```\nWe need include formatTime and _truncate_message. Construct class from snippet.\n\nNeed ensure no accidental mismatch: class ends before blank + `_root_logger`. We'll preserve.\n\nClass code:\n\n```\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an integer >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n...\n    def _truncate_message(self, message: str, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True or len(message) <= budget:\n            return message\n        marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n        remaining = budget - len(marker)\n        head_len = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail_len = remaining - head_len\n        return f\"{message[:head_len]}{marker}{message[-tail_len:] if tail_len else ''}\"\n```\nCareful if tail_len 0 but budget min32 -> remaining>=17 tail>=8, no issue. But `message[-0:]` = whole string, so guard good. `message[-tail_len:]`.\nIn format:\n```\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        full_message = record.getMessage()\n        message = self._truncate_message(full_message, record)\n        if message is not full_message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = message\n            record.args = None\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n...\n```\nUsing `is not` to detect truncation. If full_message and message same object? `_truncate_message` returns message argument, so yes same. If keep true returns same. If no truncation returns same. Good. If truncated copy.\nBut prefix: `msg.find(message)` may be -1 if message empty or message contains something transformed? Format string `%message` typically exact. If message empty, skip. For truncated message with newlines, record copied msg has truncated. Fine.\nException: `super().format(record)` on copy with `record.exc_info` original. If original record had `exc_text` maybe not; copy. Does not mutate original. If full handler later formats original, full message preserved.\n`record.getMessage()` on original may set nothing, no mutation. Good.\nPotential record.msg may be non-str object; getMessage returns str.\n\nBut `formatTime` returns base; on copy same.\n\nDefault `_setup_logger` add `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`.\n\nPotential issue if config env var invalid at import, `_setup_logger()` called at module import raises ValueError. That's intended when set invalid? Probably tests may import with invalid env and expect ValueError. fine.\n\nStats: replace `logger.info(m)` inside _log_worker only. There may be other `logger.info(m)` not. Use exact:\n```\nfor m in msgs:\n    logger.info(m)\n```\nto add extra. Also maybe `logger.info` in queue consumer elsewhere? Evidence says _log_worker. Good.\n\nPotential stats integration test extracts function source and exec? It expects `extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}` literal maybe. Good.\n\nThen run tests. `run_command([\"python\",\"-m\",\"pytest\",\"-q\"]